</ul>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'What Is THC?',
    html: `<p>THC, or delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol, is the primary psychoactive compound in cannabis. It's the one most people are referring to when they talk about getting high. When THC enters your system, it binds to CB1 receptors in the brain and central nervous system, part of the body's endocannabinoid system. This binding produces the characteristic effects: altered mood, relaxed body sensation, changed perception of time, and in some cases, increased appetite.</p>
<p>The intensity of those effects depends almost entirely on dose. At very high doses, say 10mg or more in an edible, the experience can be overwhelming for most people, particularly first-timers. At much lower doses, such as the up to 1.6mg THC per piece found in Thai FDA-approved microdose edibles like Baked Brand, the effects are subtle and manageable.</p>
<p>In Thailand, THC edibles are permitted when they carry Thai FDA approval and meet microdose limits. Baked Brand's Chocolate Chip Cookie is currently the only edible product to hold that approval, and each piece contains up to 1.6mg THC.</p>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'What Is THCA?',
    html: `<p>THCA, or tetrahydrocannabinolic acid, is the raw, unheated precursor to THC. It's the form THC takes while the cannabis plant is still growing. Fresh, unprocessed cannabis doesn't actually contain significant amounts of THC, it contains THCA. The plant produces THCA through a biosynthesis pathway starting from cannabigerolic acid (CBGA), driven by specialised plant enzymes.</p>
<p>The critical point: THCA is non-psychoactive. In its raw acidic form, THCA has very poor affinity for CB1 and CB2 receptors in the brain. Research in the pharmacology literature shows that THCA interacts with different receptor pathways, specifically PPARgamma receptors and TRPM8 ion channels, and does not produce intoxicating effects in humans. Eating raw cannabis or drinking raw cannabis juice will not get you high, because the THCA hasn't been converted to THC.</p>
<p>Preliminary research has also identified potential therapeutic properties of THCA itself, including anti-inflammatory, anti-nausea, and neuroprotective effects, though these are still early-stage findings and not confirmed in large human trials.</p>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'The Key Difference: Decarboxylation',
    html: `<p>The single thing that separates THCA from THC is heat, specifically a chemical process called decarboxylation.</p>
<p>Decarboxylation happens when THCA is exposed to sufficient heat. The heat causes the molecule to lose its carboxyl group (COOH), essentially knocking a carbon dioxide molecule off the compound. What remains is THC: the same carbon skeleton, minus the acidic group, and now capable of binding to CB1 receptors and producing psychoactive effects.</p>
<p>Research published in the Journal of Chromatography A (Wang et al., 2016) found that maximum decarboxylation efficiency occurs between 105°C and 120°C (around 220°F to 248°F). The practical sweet spot used in food manufacturing is approximately 115°C (240°F) for 30 to 40 minutes, converting the majority of THCA to THC while minimising degradation of the resulting compound.</p>
<p>This is why the three most common methods of consuming cannabis all involve heat:</p>
<ul>
<li>Smoking or vaping instantly decarboxylates THCA as it combusts or vaporises, converting it to THC in seconds.</li>
<li>Edibles are made using cannabis that has been heated (decarboxylated) during manufacturing. The THCA has already become THC before the product reaches you.</li>
<li>Raw cannabis products, juices, capsules made from fresh leaf, smoothies, retain the THCA form. These do not produce psychoactive effects.</li>
</ul>
<p>At Baked Brand, our cookies and caramels are produced using decarboxylated cannabis. By the time the product reaches you, the conversion has already happened under controlled, lab-tested conditions, so you know exactly what you're getting, in an amount (up to 1.6mg THC per piece) that's approved by the Thai FDA.</p>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'Does THCA Get You High?',
    html: `<p>No, not in its raw form. THCA on its own does not produce a psychoactive high. This is the most important practical distinction between the two compounds.</p>
<p>If you were to eat a raw cannabis leaf straight from the plant, you would not get high. The THCA hasn't been converted. There's nothing in that leaf capable of binding to the CB1 receptors in your brain in a meaningful way.</p>
<p>Once THCA is exposed to heat, however, it becomes THC. So while THCA in its raw form is not psychoactive, it is the direct precursor to the compound that is. Any product made with heated or processed cannabis that started as THCA will contain THC once it reaches your hands.</p>
<p>There's also an important note for anyone subject to drug testing: THCA that converts to THC, or that was already converted before consumption, will produce the same THC metabolites that standard drug tests detect. If you consume a heated cannabis product containing converted THC, it is detectable. This holds true for Thai FDA-approved microdose edibles as well, even at 1.6mg per piece.</p>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'What Does 1.6mg THC Actually Feel Like?',
    html: `<p>If you've never tried a THC edible before, the dosage numbers can be hard to contextualise. Here's a frame of reference that helps.</p>
<p>A standard cannabis edible sold in markets like the US or Canada typically contains 5mg to 10mg of THC per serving. Many experienced users take 10mg or more. For someone who has never tried cannabis before, 10mg is often far too much. The effects come on strong, last for hours, and can produce anxiety, disorientation, and an experience that puts many people off ever trying it again.</p>
<p>At up to 1.6mg THC per piece, which is what you get with Baked Brand's Thai FDA-approved Chocolate Chip Cookie or Cannabis Caramels (available in Mango, Vanilla, and Chocolate), you're in very different territory. A microdose at this level produces a subtle, manageable effect: mild relaxation, a gentle mood lift, a soft reduction in tension. The kind of effect that allows you to stay fully functional, present, and in control.</p>
<p>This is what microdosing means in practice. It's not about getting high. It's about finding the level at which cannabis provides a soft benefit without impairment. For first-timers especially, starting at up to 1.6mg means the experience is gentle enough to be enjoyable, and controllable enough to inform your next decision.</p>
<p>If you want to understand the science behind microdosing in more depth, including how to start, how long to wait before taking more, and what to expect across multiple sessions, see our complete <a href="/thc-edibles-dosage-guide/">THC Edibles Dosage Guide</a> and <a href="/thc-edibles-beginners-guide/">THC Edibles Beginner's Guide</a>.</p>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'Is THCA or THC Legal in Thailand?',
    html: `<p>Thailand's cannabis landscape has evolved significantly since 2022. As of current regulations, cannabis products must hold Thai FDA approval to be sold legally as food items, and recreational use remains restricted.</p>
<p>Baked Brand holds Thai FDA approval for its Chocolate Chip Cookie and Cannabis Caramels, making them the only edible products in Thailand to carry this designation. Each piece contains up to 1.6mg THC, packaging carries required consumption warnings, and products are manufactured in our own licensed factory. What's on the label reflects what's in the product. No surprises.</p>
<p>Cannabis laws in Thailand continue to evolve, and the right source for current regulatory guidance is always the official Thai FDA. For a full breakdown of what the law currently says about THC edibles, see our guide on <a href="/cannabis-legal-thailand/">Thai cannabis regulations and THC edibles</a>.</p>
<p>What we can say clearly: Baked Brand exists within an approved, compliant framework. If you're in Thailand and want to experience the gentle effects of a microdose THC edible without legal ambiguity, our products were specifically created and approved to fill that space.</p>`,
  },
  {
    h2: 'THCA vs THC: At a Glance',
    html: `<div class="bk-table-wrap" style="overflow-x:auto">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>THCA</th><th>THC</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Full name</td><td>Tetrahydrocannabinolic acid</td><td>Delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol</td></tr>
<tr><td>Found in</td><td>Raw, unheated cannabis</td><td>Heated / decarboxylated cannabis</td></tr>
<tr><td>Psychoactive?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
<tr><td>Binds CB1 receptors?</td><td>Poorly</td><td>Strongly</td></tr>
<tr><td>Converts to THC?</td><td>Yes, via heat (decarboxylation)</td><td>N/A</td></tr>
<tr><td>Triggers drug test?</td><td>Unlikely if consumed raw</td><td>Yes, including microdose edibles</td></tr>
<tr><td>In Baked Brand products?</td><td>No, pre-decarboxylated</td><td>Yes, up to 1.6mg per piece</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>`,
