  {
    h2: 'Frequently Asked Questions',
    html: `<h3>Is THCA the same as THC?</h3>
<p>No. THCA (tetrahydrocannabinolic acid) is the non-psychoactive, raw precursor to THC found in the cannabis plant before heat is applied. THC (delta-9-tetrahydrocannabinol) is the psychoactive compound that THCA converts into during decarboxylation. They share the same carbon skeleton but differ by a single carboxyl group, and that difference determines whether the compound is psychoactive or not.</p>

<h3>Does THCA show up on a drug test?</h3>
<p>It depends on whether the THCA has been heated. Raw THCA consumed without heat, such as in cannabis juice, is unlikely to produce a positive drug test result, because it is not metabolised into THC in the body. If THCA has been converted to THC through heating, as in any cooked or baked edible, the resulting THC metabolites will be detectable on standard drug tests. Consuming any heated cannabis product, including microdose edibles, can result in a positive test.</p>

<h3>What is the difference between THCA and CBD?</h3>
<p>THCA is the precursor to THC and becomes psychoactive once heated. CBD (cannabidiol) is an entirely different cannabinoid with a separate biosynthesis pathway. CBD does not convert to THC when heated, and it is not psychoactive regardless of how it is processed. Both are found in the cannabis plant, but they have very different effects on the human body.</p>

<h3>Can you get high from raw cannabis?</h3>
<p>No. Raw, unheated cannabis contains THCA rather than THC. Because THCA cannot bind effectively to CB1 receptors in the brain, eating raw cannabis leaves or drinking raw cannabis juice will not produce a psychoactive high. The psychoactive effect only occurs after decarboxylation, the conversion of THCA to THC through heat.</p>

<h3>Is THCA legal in Thailand?</h3>
<p>Thailand's cannabis regulations focus on THC content and product type rather than specifically on THCA. Products containing THC require Thai FDA approval. THCA in raw plant form falls under broader cannabis regulations. For the most current guidance on legal status, always refer to official Thai FDA and government sources, as the regulatory framework continues to evolve.</p>

<h3>What happens when you heat THCA?</h3>
<p>When THCA is exposed to sufficient heat, around 105°C to 120°C (220°F to 248°F), it undergoes decarboxylation. The molecule loses its carboxyl group (COOH), releasing CO2, and transforms into delta-9-THC. This newly formed THC can then bind to CB1 receptors in the brain, producing psychoactive effects. This is why smoking, vaping, and cooking cannabis all activate the compound, they all apply enough heat to trigger this conversion.</p>

<h3>How long does it take for a THC edible to kick in?</h3>
<p>THC edibles typically take 30 to 90 minutes to produce noticeable effects, though some people don't feel anything for up to two hours. This delay occurs because THC must be digested, absorbed through the gut, processed by the liver, and then reach the brain via the bloodstream. The most common mistake with edibles is taking a second piece because "it's not working" before the first dose has had time to kick in. Start with one piece and set a 90-minute timer before reassessing.</p>`,
  },
];
